---
title: "Coming from Quarto"
description: "What carries over untouched, the handful of things that do not, and why `taliesin check` is the migration assistant rather than this page."
---

Taliesin renders the same Markdown you already write. If you are arriving from a
Quarto (or R Markdown, or plain Pandoc) project, the sentences, the fenced code
cells, the `#|` cell options, the `:::` fenced divs, the `$…$` math, the `[@key]`
citations and the `@fig-`/`@sec-` cross-references all come across **unchanged**.
What differs is a short, closed list of *vocabulary*, and you do not have to learn
it from this page:

```sh
taliesin check .
```

`check` reads your project and reports every one of these by name, with a file and
a line, so a migration is a loop of "run check, fix what it points at" rather than
a reading exercise. This chapter is the map of what it will find.

That "unchanged" is a measured claim rather than a reassurance, and the measurement is in
@sec-portability: across the whole corpus, 7.3% of lines carry any construct beyond plain
CommonMark. It is also the number to read before migrating *away* again — the same 92.7% is
what any other Markdown tool would get back.

## Step 1: rename the files

The one thing no tool can do for you.

| Quarto | Taliesin |
|---|---|
| `document.qmd` | `document.tmd` |
| `_quarto.yml` | `_site.yml` |

`.tmd` is the only accepted source extension. A directory that still holds a
`_quarto.yml` is **named, not silently ignored** — `check` says so and tells you
what to rename it to.

Then your internal links still point at the old extension, which is the single
biggest source of noise in a fresh migration: on two real books, **118 of 123** and
**10 of 11** link errors were exactly this one shape. Taliesin recognises a link
whose target ends in `.md`, `.markdown`, `.qmd`, `.rmd`, `.Rmd` or `.ipynb` and
suggests the `.tmd` spelling next door. It **suggests** rather than rewrites: a
`.md` link may point at a real `.md` file you ship on purpose.

## Step 2: the front matter

Every unknown top-level key warns with a did-you-mean, so a typo and a
Quarto-ism are told apart. These are the ones with a specific answer.

### `format:` names an output, and the output is HTML

Taliesin renders **HTML only**. There is no PDF, LaTeX, Typst, Word or ePub target
and there is not going to be one; a print track, if it ever exists, would render
*from* the built HTML rather than alongside it. So `format:` takes a name —
`html` (the default) or `deck` — and these values are reported as unsupported
rather than quietly producing an HTML page you did not ask for:

`pdf` · `typst` · `docx` · `latex` · `beamer` · `epub` · `pptx` · `odt` · `rtf` ·
`jats` · `docbook`

Two more `format:` shapes to know:

- **`format: revealjs`** (and any `*-revealjs`) → **`format: deck`**. Decks run on
  Taliesin's own engine; reveal.js is not vendored, not fetched, and not a
  fallback.
- **`format:` sub-keys are read by nothing.** `format:\n  html:\n    toc: true`
  looks like it works and does not. Move the key to the top level (`toc: true`);
  `check` says which one you meant when the sub-key names a real top-level key.

### Keys that were removed or are not honoured

| You wrote | What happens |
|---|---|
| `csl: apa.csl` | Recognised but **not** honoured. References always render in the built-in IEEE style; the `.csl` file is never parsed. |
| `about:` | Removed on 2026-07-17. The landing banner is `hero:`, which takes different sub-keys (`eyebrow` / `headline` / `lead` / `actions`) — a rewrite, not a rename. |
| `theorems: { number-within: … }` | Removed. Theorem numbers scope to a numbered book chapter automatically, so nothing replaces it: delete the key. |
| `theorems: { numbered: … }` | Removed on 2026-08-02. Environments are numbered; `shared:` (one counter across several kinds) is the whole block now. |
| `css:`, `include-in-header:`, `include-before-body:`, `include-after-body:` | Removed on 2026-08-02, along with `_site.yml`'s `css:`, `body-start:` and `body-end:`. One project-wide hatch replaces all seven: `_site.yml` `head:`, which injects markup into every page's `<head>`. For colours and type prefer a `theme:`. |
| `execute: { echo: … }`, `execute: { include: … }` | Removed on 2026-08-02. Say it on the cells you mean: `#| echo: false` in the cell. `execute: { cache: … }` remains, because caching is genuinely a whole-document property. |
| `listing: { sort: … }` | Removed on 2026-08-02. A listing is newest-first by `date`, which is what every listing that spelled it out was asking for. |
| `listing: { categories: … }` | Removed on 2026-08-03. It rendered a filter-chip row above the cards; the row paid off only on a large archive with a disciplined category vocabulary. Page-level `categories:` still works and still shows as a badge on each card. |
| `hero: { image: … }`, `hero: { image-alt: … }` | Removed on 2026-08-02. The banner is type, not a figure; put the image in the page body as a normal figure. |
| `prose-lint:` | Removed on 2026-08-02 with the linter it configured (doubled words, weasel words, `banned:`). Nothing replaces it: delete the key. |
| `doi:`, `venue:`, `award:`, `links:`, `acknowledgments:` (and `acknowledgements:`) | Removed on 2026-08-03 with the "Cite this" box, the resource row and the Google Scholar `citation_*` metadata they fed. Citations are untouched — `bibliography:` and `[@key]` stay. Write a DOI or an artefact link as an ordinary Markdown link in the body, name the venue in the body or in `subtitle:`, and write acknowledgements as a closing section, where you can edit them. |
| `datasets:` | Removed on 2026-08-02, and the `{{< dataset >}}` shortcode its annotations moved onto followed on 2026-08-03. Cite a data file the way you cite anything else: link it from the prose, and let a `bibliography:` entry carry the provenance if you need one. |
| `page-layout: article` (or `custom`, …) | Ignored. Taliesin uses the reading-width column by default and only `page-layout: full` widens it. |

### `_site.yml` keys that were removed

| You wrote | What happens |
|---|---|
| `output: _site` | Removed on 2026-08-02. `build` writes `_site/` (`_book/` for a book); use `build --out <dir>` to write elsewhere. |
| `toc: true` | Removed on 2026-08-02. The sidebar table of contents is automatic: an article page with enough headings gets one. To force it for a page, write `toc:` in that page's own front matter. |
| `css:`, `body-start:`, `body-end:` | Removed on 2026-08-02. Use `head:`, the one surviving raw-injection key. |
| `theorems:` | Removed on 2026-08-02 with the book-wide numbering policy it carried. A chapter that shares counters says so itself: `theorems: { shared: [...] }`. |
| `mounts: - { at:, path: }` | The list form was removed on 2026-08-02. Write `mounts:` as a mapping of URL prefix to project directory: `mounts:` / `  docs: ../docs`. |

None of these is phrased as a did-you-mean on purpose. A did-you-mean is a
mechanical rename, and a tool (or an agent) will apply it mechanically — but
`hero:` is not `about:` with a new name, so a blind rewrite would trade a warning
for a document that is wrong in a new way.

## Step 3: the body

Almost everything is untouched. Two habits worth naming:

- **`::: {.columns}` with `.column` children** — Taliesin has one column grid and it
  is the bare attribute: `::: {layout-ncol=2}`, whose direct child blocks are the
  columns. There is no per-column fence, so there is also no per-column `width=`;
  widths are equal by construction. `check` says a leftover `.columns` was *removed*
  rather than guessing at a rename, because the rewrite deletes the child fences
  instead of renaming them.
- **`::: {.callout-important}` / `::: {.callout-caution}`** — Taliesin's callout
  vocabulary is three kinds: `note:` an aside, `tip:` advice, `warning:` a consequence
  the reader should not miss. `important:` and `caution:` were retired on 2026-08-03 —
  a reader could not reliably tell either apart from `warning:` by sight, so all three
  were the same coloured box with a different word in it. `check` says a leftover one
  was *removed* rather than guessing at a rename, since the replacement is a judgement
  call (which of `warning`/`note` fits what the callout actually says), not a mechanical
  one.

Cell options (`#| echo: false`, `#| label: fig-…`, `#| fig-cap: "…"`) work as you
expect, and the document-level `execute:` block honours `echo`, `include` and
`cache`; anything else under it warns with the closest match.

## What this buys you

Everything above is a diagnostic with a file and a line, which is the actual
difference: a migration finishes when `check` exits 0, and you can see the whole
distance from the first run. Run it before you start reading anything:

```sh
taliesin check .          # a project
taliesin check page.tmd   # one document
taliesin build . --strict # ...and make a warning fail the build once you are clean
```

See [Command-line reference](../reference/cli.tmd) for the rest of `check`, and
[Front matter](../reference/frontmatter.tmd) for the full key vocabulary.
